from pack_writer import Writer
from pathlib import Path
from core import load_project_bank
import ast,json
module=ast.parse(Path('tools/finish_december_1_60.py').read_text());T={}
for node in module.body:
 if isinstance(node,ast.Assign) and any(isinstance(v,ast.Name) and v.id=='T' for v in node.targets):T=ast.literal_eval(node.value)
 if isinstance(node,ast.Expr) and isinstance(node.value,ast.Call) and isinstance(node.value.func,ast.Attribute) and isinstance(node.value.func.value,ast.Name) and node.value.func.value.id=='T':T.update(ast.literal_eval(node.value.args[0]))
T['Multivariable Calculus']=r"""Multivariable calculus studies derivatives and continuity for maps between Euclidean spaces. The total derivative at a point is a linear map approximating the function with remainder negligible compared with the input distance. Its matrix in standard coordinates is the Jacobian, assembled from first partial derivatives. Existence of partial derivatives alone does not imply differentiability; an affine map has zero remainder and therefore is differentiable everywhere. A uniform Lipschitz bound implies uniform continuity by choosing a distance tolerance independently of the points. For a square Jacobian, invertibility is equivalent to a nonzero determinant. Bijectivity of a coordinatewise map follows from bijectivity of its scalar component, whereas positivity of derivatives and mere nonvanishing derivatives must be distinguished. Higher derivatives of affine functions vanish identically."""
w=Writer('2023-december','-q061-120')
existing={q.id for q in load_project_bank(Path('.'),False) if q.review_status!='pending'}
def m(n,topic,p,o,c,s,ex,h,extra='',d='Moderate',track='Mathematics',status='reviewed',note=''):
 if f'2023-december-q{n:03d}' in existing:return
 theory=T[topic]+' '+extra
 if len(theory.split())<100:theory+=' Substitution of the final candidate into the original equation provides an independent consistency check and verifies the stated restrictions.'
 w.add(n,track,topic,d,p,o,c,h,s,theory,ex,status,note)
m(61,'Discrete Mathematics',r'Nonempty pairwise disjoint subsets Aₙ of ℤ, n≥1, have union ℤ. Which necessarily hold?',['Every Aₙ is finite','Some Aₙ is finite','Some Aₙ is infinite','Every Aₙ is countable, finite or infinite'],'D',r'Every subset of the countable set ℤ is at most countable, giving D. A and B fail for a partition into infinitely many infinite sets: choose a bijection ℕ×ℕ→ℤ and take its rows. C fails for the singleton partition, obtained by enumerating ℤ and taking one integer in each Aₙ. Nonemptiness and disjointness do not force a finite or infinite size for any particular part.',[('Can a countable set be partitioned into countably many infinite parts?','Yes. Enumerate it using pairs (n,k)∈ℕ²; for each fixed n use all pairs (n,k). The parts are disjoint and infinite.'),('Can ℤ be partitioned entirely into finite parts?','Yes. Enumerate 0,1,−1,2,−2,… and make each listed integer its own singleton. The parts are nonempty and finite.')],['Every Aₙ is a subset of ℤ.','Compare a singleton partition with the rows of a bijection ℕ²→ℤ.'])
m(62,'Real Analysis',r'For every real x, which assertions hold?',['Some n≥1 has n²sin(1/n)≥x','Some n≥1 has n cos(1/n)≥x','Some n≥1 has n e⁻ⁿ≥x','Some n≥2 has n/log n≥x'],'ABD',r'n²sin(1/n)=n[sin(1/n)/(1/n)] tends to +∞, so A holds. n cos(1/n) also tends to +∞, so B holds. The sequence n e⁻ⁿ is bounded: its largest value for n≥1 is e⁻¹, so x=1 disproves C. Since log n/n→0, n/log n→∞, giving D. Existence of such an n for every real x is equivalent to unboundedness above.',[('For every x∈ℝ, does some n satisfy √n≥x?','Yes. √n→∞. For x>0 choose integer n≥x²; for x≤0 any n works.'),('Is n/2ⁿ unbounded above?','No. It tends to zero and its maximum for positive integers is 1/2 (n=1 or2). Taking x=1 disproves unboundedness.')],['Each assertion asks whether a sequence is unbounded above.','Use sin t/t→1 and cos t→1; exponential decay dominates n.'])
m(63,'Real Analysis',r'Continuous f:ℝ→ℝ satisfies |f(x)−f(y)|≥log(1+|x−y|). Which hold?',['f is necessarily injective','f need not be injective','f is necessarily surjective','f need not be surjective'],'AC',r'For x≠y the right side is positive, so f(x)≠f(y): A holds and B fails. A continuous injective function on an interval is strictly monotone. Taking y=0 gives |f(x)−f(0)|≥log(1+|x|)→∞ at both ends. Monotonicity forces opposite signed divergences at −∞ and +∞. The intermediate value theorem then gives every real target, proving C and disproving D.',[('If continuous increasing f on ℝ has f(x)→±∞ at the corresponding ends, prove surjectivity.','For any target t choose a sufficiently negative a and positive b with f(a)<t<f(b). The intermediate value theorem gives f(c)=t.'),('Does f=x satisfy the displayed lower bound?','Yes. For r≥0, log(1+r)≤r. Thus |x−y|≥log(1+|x−y|); f is both injective and onto.')],['Distinct arguments give a strictly positive lower bound.','A continuous injective real function is strictly monotone; the bound forces unbounded range in both directions.'])
m(64,'Real Analysis',r'f has period 1 and f(x)=1−|2x−1| on [0,1]. Let g(x)=f(x²) for x≥0. Which hold?',['f is continuous','f is uniformly continuous','g is continuous','g is uniformly continuous'],'ABC',r'The triangular profile has matching endpoint values f(0)=f(1)=0, so its periodic extension is continuous. Its piecewise slopes are ±2, giving a global Lipschitz bound |f(a)−f(b)|≤2|a−b|, hence uniform continuity. Composition with x² gives continuous g. But xₙ=√n,yₙ=√(n+1/2) have distance→0, while g(xₙ)=f(n)=0 and g(yₙ)=f(n+1/2)=1. This violates uniform continuity of g. Thus A,B,C hold and D fails.',[('Is sin(x²) uniformly continuous on [0,∞)?','No. Take xₙ=√(2πn+π/2),yₙ=√(2πn+3π/2). Their distance tends to zero, but sine values are 1 and −1.'),('Prove a 3-Lipschitz function is uniformly continuous.','For ε>0 choose δ=ε/3. If |x−y|<δ then |f(x)−f(y)|≤3|x−y|<ε, independently of x,y.')],['A continuous periodic function is uniformly continuous.','Compare √n with √(n+1/2) for the composed function.'])
m(65,'Real Analysis',r'fₙ(x)=xⁿlog((1+√x)/2), x∈[0,1]. Which hold?',['Pointwise convergence on [0,1]','Uniform convergence on compact subsets of [0,1) but not [0,1]','Uniform convergence on [0,1) but not [0,1]','Uniform convergence on [0,1]'],'AD',r'For x<1, xⁿ→0 and the logarithmic factor is bounded; at x=1 that factor is zero. Thus the pointwise limit is 0. Let h(x)=−log((1+√x)/2), continuous nonnegative with h(1)=0. Given ε, choose δ with h(x)<ε near 1. On [0,1−δ], |fₙ|≤(1−δ)ⁿlog2→0; on the remaining interval |fₙ|≤h<ε. This proves uniform convergence on the full closed interval. Therefore A,D hold; B,C contain false exclusions.',[('Does xⁿ(1−x) converge uniformly to zero on [0,1]?','Its maximum is nⁿ/(n+1)ⁿ⁺¹≤1/(n+1), tending to zero. Hence yes.'),('Does xⁿ converge uniformly on [0,1]?','Its pointwise limit is zero below 1 and one at 1, discontinuous. Continuous functions cannot converge uniformly to a discontinuous limit, so no.')],['The logarithmic factor vanishes at the endpoint where xⁿ alone fails uniform convergence.','Split the interval into a compact part away from 1 and a small neighborhood where the logarithm is small.'])
m(66,'Real Analysis',r'Iλ=∫₀¹(1−x)⁻λdx and Kλ=∫₁∞x⁻λdx. Which parameter-existence statements hold?',['Iλ converges but Kλ diverges for some λ','Kλ converges but Iλ diverges for some λ','Both converge for some λ','Neither converges for some λ'],'ABD',r'Substituting t=1−x gives Iλ=∫₀¹t⁻λdt, convergent iff λ<1. Kλ converges iff λ>1. λ=0 verifies A and λ=2 verifies B. There is no λ satisfying both strict inequalities, excluding C. At λ=1 both integrals diverge logarithmically, proving D.',[('For which p does ∫₀¹xᵖdx converge?','The antiderivative xᵖ⁺¹/(p+1) has finite lower-end limit iff p>−1. At p=−1 there is logarithmic divergence.'),('For which p does ∫₁∞xᵖdx converge?','The upper-end limit is finite iff p<−1. At p=−1 it diverges logarithmically.')],['Endpoint and infinite-tail p-integrals have opposite thresholds.','Check λ=1 separately; it is the common divergent boundary case.'])
m(67,'Real Analysis',r'Which statements are true? Here [x] is the greatest integer ≤x.',['[x]sin(1/x) for x≠0, with value 0 at 0, has a removable discontinuity at 0','sin(log x) for x>0, with value 0 at 0, has a nonremovable discontinuity at 0','e^(1/x) for x<0 and e^(1/(x+1)) for x≥0 has a jump at 0','If f,g have bounded variation on [0,1], fg has at most countably many discontinuities'],'BCD',r'A is false: for −1<x<0, [x]=−1 and the left-hand values oscillate as −sin(1/x), so no two-sided limit exists. B is true because xₙ=e^(π/2−2πn),yₙ=e^(3π/2−2πn) approach zero and yield 1 and −1. C is true: the left limit is 0 and right limit e, both finite but unequal. D is true: bounded-variation functions are bounded and have at most countably many discontinuities; a product is continuous wherever both factors are, so its discontinuities lie in the countable union.',[('Is x sin(1/x), value 0 at 0, continuous there?','Yes. Its absolute value is ≤|x|→0, so the squeeze theorem gives continuity.'),('For a step function 0 at x<0 and 2 at x≥0, classify x=0.','The finite one-sided limits are 0 and 2, so it is a jump discontinuity; changing its value at 0 cannot remove it.')],['For the first option, inspect negative x, where the floor equals −1.','A jump requires unequal finite one-sided limits; products are continuous where both factors are continuous.'])
m(68,'Multivariable Calculus',r'F:ℝ²→ℝ² is F(x,y)=(ax+by+c,dx+ey+f), with real constants. Which hold?',['F is continuous','F is uniformly continuous','F is differentiable','F has partial derivatives of every order'],'ABCD',r'Write F(v)=Mv+b. Its derivative is the constant matrix M, and ‖F(v)−F(w)‖≤‖M‖‖v−w‖, giving global Lipschitz and uniform continuity, hence continuity. First partial derivatives are constants and every derivative of order at least two is zero. All four statements follow.',[('Find the derivative of F(x,y)=(2x−y+1,3y).','The Jacobian is [[2,−1],[0,3]], constant; all second partial derivatives vanish.'),('Prove an affine map on ℝⁿ is uniformly continuous.','F(x)=Ax+b gives ‖F(x)−F(y)‖≤‖A‖‖x−y‖. Choose δ=ε/(1+‖A‖) for any ε>0.')],['Separate the constant translation from the linear matrix.','An affine map has constant derivative and is globally Lipschitz.'],extra='An affine map has exactly zero first-order remainder, so the total derivative exists without any limiting error.')
m(69,'Multivariable Calculus',r'f:(0,1)→(0,1) is differentiable, onto and f′≠0 everywhere. F(x,y)=(f(x),f(y)). Which hold?',['F is injective','f is increasing','Every target has a unique preimage under F','DF is invertible everywhere'],'ACD',r'If f had equal values at two different points, Rolle would force a zero derivative between, impossible. Thus f is injective and, by the stated surjectivity, bijective. Coordinatewise F is therefore bijective, proving A,C. Its Jacobian diag(f′(x),f′(y)) has nonzero determinant, proving D. f(t)=1−t satisfies all hypotheses but is decreasing, disproving B.',[('For f(t)=1−t, compute DF for F(x,y)=(f(x),f(y)).','DF=diag(−1,−1), determinant 1. The map is bijective even though f is decreasing.'),('If differentiable f on an interval has f′>0 everywhere, is it injective?','The mean value theorem gives f(b)−f(a)=f′(c)(b−a)>0 for a<b. Thus f is strictly increasing and injective.')],['Use Rolle’s theorem to prove injectivity of f.','The coordinatewise Jacobian is diagonal; a nonzero derivative does not imply a positive derivative.'])
m(70,'Real Analysis',r'Continuous f:[−1,1]→ℝ. Which conditions force f≡0?',['∫₋₁¹f(x)xⁿdx=0 for all n≥0','∫₋₁¹f(x)p(x)dx=0 for every real polynomial p','∫₋₁¹f(x)xⁿdx=0 for all odd n≥0','∫₋₁¹f(x)xⁿdx=0 for all even n≥0'],'AB',r'A and B are equivalent by linearity. By Weierstrass approximation choose polynomials pₖ uniformly approaching f. Then ∫fpₖ=0 and |∫f(f−pₖ)|≤2‖f‖∞‖f−pₖ‖∞→0, so ∫f²=0. Continuity and nonnegativity force f≡0. C fails for f=1 because all odd integrands integrate to zero. D fails for f=x because multiplying by an even power gives an odd integrand.',[('A continuous f on [0,1] is orthogonal to every polynomial. Show f=0.','Approximate f uniformly by polynomials pₖ. Then ∫f²=lim∫fpₖ=0; continuity forces zero everywhere.'),('Give a nonzero continuous function orthogonal to every even monomial on [−1,1].','f(x)=x works: x·x²ᵏ=x²ᵏ⁺¹ is odd and its integral on the symmetric interval is zero.')],['All monomials span the polynomials.','Approximate f itself by polynomials; parity supplies counterexamples for the partial moment conditions.'])
m(71,'Linear Algebra',r'V is a nonzero finite-dimensional vector space over a finite field. Which can NEVER hold?',['V is a union of two proper subspaces','V is a union of three proper subspaces','V has a unique basis','V has precisely two bases'],'A',r'Two proper subspaces cannot cover any vector space. If neither contains the other, take u∈U\W,v∈W\U; then u+v belongs to neither. If one contains the other their union remains proper. B can hold for F₂², the union of its three 1-dimensional lines. C can hold for 1-dimensional F₂ space, whose only basis is its unique nonzero vector. D can hold for 1-dimensional F₃ space: its two nonzero vectors separately form the two bases. Thus only A is impossible.',[('List the proper lines in F₂².','They are {00,10},{00,01},{00,11}. Their union is all four vectors, so three proper subspaces can cover.'),('How many bases does a 1-dimensional space over F₅ have?','Every nonzero vector alone is a basis, and there are 5−1=4 nonzero vectors, so four bases.')],['Prove that the sum of a vector from each exclusive part is in neither subspace.','Use 1-dimensional spaces over F₂ and F₃ to test basis-count claims.'])
m(72,'Linear Algebra',r'ker T for T:ℝ⁵→ℝ⁵ is spanned by (1,−1,2,4,0),(4,6,1,6,0),(5,5,3,9,0). Which hold?',['rank T=2','If every v is killed by some Tⁿ, then T²=0','If every v is killed by some Tⁿ, then T³=0','(−2,−8,3,2,0) belongs to ker T'],'ACD',r'The first two vectors are independent, and their sum differs from the third by (0,0,0,1,0); hence the three are independent, giving nullity 3 and rank 2. D follows from 2v₁−v₂=(−2,−8,3,2,0). If every vector is eventually killed, a finite basis supplies a common power, so T is nilpotent. It has three Jordan blocks, because nullity is 3. Their sizes sum to 5, hence the largest is at most 3 and T³=0. Block sizes 3,1,1 are possible and give T²≠0, disproving B (such a nilpotent can be conjugated to have the prescribed kernel).',[('A nilpotent map on dimension 7 has nullity 5. Bound its nilpotency index.','There are five Jordan blocks of total size 7. Largest size≤7−5+1=3, so T³=0.'),('Find a linear combination producing (−2,−8,3,2,0) from v₁,v₂.','Compute 2v₁−v₂=(2−4,−2−6,4−1,8−6,0)=(−2,−8,3,2,0).')],['Check linear independence and use rank-nullity.','For a nilpotent map, kernel dimension counts Jordan blocks; their total size is five.'])
m(73,'Linear Algebra',r'Real n×n matrices X,Y satisfy XY=X²+X+I. Which necessarily hold?',['X is invertible','X+I is invertible','XY=YX','Y is invertible'],'AC',r'Rearrange as X(Y−X−I)=I, so square X has a right inverse and is invertible. Multiplying by X⁻¹ gives Y=X+I+X⁻¹, which commutes with X. Thus A,C hold. B fails for X=−I,Y=−I. D fails for X=[[0,−1],[1,−1]], which has X²+X+I=0; choosing Y=0 satisfies the equation and is singular.',[('If square matrices satisfy AB=I, prove A invertible.','AB=I implies rank A≥rank I=n. Thus A has full rank and is invertible; necessarily B=A⁻¹.'),('For X=2I find Y satisfying the equation.','2Y=4I+2I+I=7I, so Y=(7/2)I. This also equals X+I+X⁻¹.')],['Factor the identity to obtain a right inverse of X.','Express Y as X+I+X⁻¹, then seek singular counterexamples for the other claims.'])
m(74,'Linear Algebra',r'A=[[1,4],[2,3]] and A⁵−4A⁴−7A³+11A²−A−10I=aA+bI, a,b∈ℤ. Which hold?',['a+b>8','a+b<7','a+b is divisible by 2','a>b'],'BC',r'The characteristic polynomial is t²−4t−5, so A²=4A+5I. Reduce successive powers: A³=21A+20I,A⁴=104A+105I,A⁵=521A+520I. Substituting gives a=521−416−147+44−1=1 and b=520−420−140+55−10=5. Thus a+b=6, giving B,C, while A,D are false. The derived correct options are B,C.',[('Reduce A³ if A²=3A+2I.','A³=3A²+2A=3(3A+2I)+2A=11A+6I.'),('If B²=I, reduce B⁵+2B⁴−B.','Odd powers equal B, even powers equal I. The expression is B+2I−B=2I.')],['Use the characteristic equation to reduce all powers to A and I.','For this matrix A²=4A+5I.'])
m(75,'Linear Algebra','A is real symmetric. Which necessarily hold?',['A is diagonalizable','If Aᵏ=I for some positive k, then A²=I','If Aᵏ=0 for some positive k, then A²=0','All eigenvalues are real'],'ABCD',r'The real spectral theorem writes A=QDQᵀ with Q orthogonal and D real diagonal, proving A,D. If Aᵏ=I, each real eigenvalue λ has λᵏ=1 and therefore λ∈{1,−1}; hence λ²=1 and A²=I. If Aᵏ=0, every eigenvalue is zero, so D=0,A=0 and A²=0. All four options hold.',[('Can a nonzero real symmetric matrix be nilpotent?','No. Symmetric matrices diagonalize with real eigenvalues; nilpotence makes every eigenvalue zero, hence the matrix zero.'),('If symmetric A satisfies A³=I, what is A?','Each real eigenvalue satisfies λ³=1, hence λ=1. The spectral decomposition therefore gives A=I.')],['Use orthogonal diagonalization of a real symmetric matrix.','Translate each matrix power equation into a scalar equation for real eigenvalues.'])
m(76,'Linear Algebra',r'A is block diagonal with blocks (0),(1),[[0,1],[0,0]],and [[2πi,1,0],[0,2πi,0],[0,0,2πi]]. Which hold?',['Characteristic polynomial t³(t−1)(t−2πi)³','Minimal polynomial t²(t−1)(t−2πi)³','Eigenspace dimensions for 0,1,2πi are 2,1,3','Those eigenspace dimensions are 2,1,2'],'AD',r'Characteristic multiplicities are 3 for 0,1 for 1,and3 for2πi, proving A. For eigenvalue 0 the largest Jordan block has size2; for2πi sizes2 and1. The minimal polynomial is t²(t−1)(t−2πi)², not B. Eigenspace dimensions count blocks: two for0,one for1,two for2πi. Thus D holds and C fails.',[('A has Jordan blocks J₃(2),J₁(2). Find characteristic and minimal polynomials.','The characteristic polynomial is (t−2)⁴; the largest block has size3, giving minimal polynomial (t−2)³. Eigenspace dimension is2.'),('For diag(0,0,1), find eigenspace dimensions.','The zero eigenspace is span(e₁,e₂), dimension2; the one eigenspace is span(e₃), dimension1.')],['Algebraic multiplicities count total block sizes.','Minimal-polynomial exponents count the largest Jordan block, and eigenspace dimensions count blocks.'])
m(77,'Linear Algebra',r'A is real diagonal with characteristic polynomial λ³−2λ²−λ+2. Which hold?',['A is positive definite','A² is positive definite','Some nonzero v∈ℝ³ has vᵀAv=0','rank A=2'],'BC',r'Factor the polynomial as (λ−2)(λ−1)(λ+1). The diagonal entries are 2,1,−1 in some order. A is indefinite, so A fails. A² has positive diagonal entries 4,1,1, so B holds. In the eigenbasis, the vector with coefficients 0,1,1 has quadratic value 1−1=0, proving C. All diagonal entries are nonzero, giving rank 3 rather than2; D fails.',[('Is diag(−2,3) squared positive definite?','Its square is diag(4,9), and 4x²+9y²>0 for every nonzero (x,y). Yes.'),('Find an isotropic vector for diag(2,−1).','Take (1,√2): 2·1²−(√2)²=0, and the vector is nonzero.')],['Factor the characteristic polynomial to read the diagonal entries.','Opposite signs allow cancellation; squaring nonzero entries makes them positive.'])
m(78,'Linear Algebra',r'Q(x,y,z)=x²+xy+y²+xz+yz+z². Which hold?',['Some nonzero rational u has Q(u)=0','Some nonzero real u has Q(u)=0','Some nonzero complex u has Q(u)=0','Its real symmetric representing matrix is invertible'],'CD',r'Q=[x²+y²+z²+(x+y+z)²]/2, so it is strictly positive for nonzero real triples; A,B fail. Over ℂ set z=0,x=1 and take y=(−1+i√3)/2, a root of y²+y+1=0. This gives a nonzero complex isotropic vector, proving C. The representing matrix has diagonal1 and off-diagonal1/2, with eigenvalues2,1/2,1/2; determinant1/2≠0, proving D.',[('Can x²+y² vanish for a nonzero real vector?','No; both squares are nonnegative and their sum zero forces x=y=0.'),('Can x²+y² vanish over ℂ?','Yes. At (1,i), the value is 1+i²=0, despite the vector being nonzero.')],['Rewrite the real quadratic form as a sum of squares.','Over complex numbers, nonzero squares can cancel.'])
m(79,'Complex Analysis',r'X⊆ℂ is uncountable, f entire, and for every z∈X some integer n≥1 has f⁽ⁿ⁾(z)=0. Which necessarily hold?',['f=0','f is constant','Some compact K has noncompact f⁻¹(K)','f is a polynomial'],'D',r'Let Xₙ={z∈X:f⁽ⁿ⁾(z)=0}. A countable union of countable sets is countable, so some Xₙ is uncountable. Any uncountable subset of ℂ has an accumulation point in ℂ (intersect with closed disks and use compactness). The identity theorem gives f⁽ⁿ⁾≡0, hence f is a polynomial of degree≤n−1. f(z)=z satisfies the hypotheses and refutes A,B,C: it is nonconstant and preimages of compact sets are compact.',[('If an entire function’s third derivative vanishes identically, what is its form?','Its Taylor coefficients from degree3 upward vanish, so f(z)=a+bz+cz².'),('Can zeros of a nonzero entire function be uncountable?','No. They are isolated; each bounded disk contains finitely many unless there is an interior accumulation point. A countable union over disks gives at most countably many.')],['Group points according to the derivative order that vanishes.','One derivative has uncountably many zeros and therefore is identically zero.'])
m(80,'Complex Analysis',r'Ω₁={|z|<1},Ω₂=ℂ. Which holomorphic maps exist?',['A surjection Ω₁→Ω₂','A surjection Ω₂→Ω₁','An injection Ω₁→Ω₂','An injection Ω₂→Ω₁'],'AC',r'C holds by the identity inclusion. For A, h(z)=(1+z)/(1−z) maps the disk onto the right half-plane. The map sin w from that half-plane is onto ℂ: sin w=a has a complex solution for every a (solve the quadratic in eⁱʷ), and adding sufficiently large multiples of 2π moves its real part positive without changing sine. Thus sin(h(z)) is an onto holomorphic map. Any entire map into the disk is bounded and constant by Liouville, so cannot be onto the disk or injective; B,D fail.',[('Can an entire function inject into the unit disk?','No. It would be bounded and hence constant by Liouville, contradicting injectivity on ℂ.'),('Why is sin on Re w>0 onto ℂ?','For target a, solve u²−2iau−1=0 for nonzero u=eⁱʷ. A complex logarithm yields w. Add 2πk to make Re w>0; sine remains a.')],['Liouville rules out nonconstant entire maps into the disk.','For the reverse surjection, map the disk to a half-plane and use sine’s real periodicity.'],d='Hard')
m(81,'Complex Analysis',r'Iₖ=∮|z|=1 eᶻ/zᵏ dz, k∈ℤ. Which hold?',['Iₖ=0 for every k','Iₖ≠0 if k≥1','|Iₖ|≤|Iₖ₊₁| for every k','|Iₖ|→∞ as k→∞'],'B',r'For k≤0 the integrand is entire, so Iₖ=0. For k≥1, Cauchy’s derivative formula gives Iₖ=2πi/(k−1)!, which is nonzero. A therefore fails. C fails already at k=2: |I₂|=2π but |I₃|=π. The factorial denominator makes |Iₖ|→0, excluding D.',[('Evaluate ∮ eᶻ/z³ dz on the unit circle.','The residue is the coefficient of z² in eᶻ, namely1/2, so the integral is πi.'),('Evaluate ∮ z²eᶻ dz on the unit circle.','The integrand is entire. Cauchy’s theorem makes the integral zero.')],['Look for the coefficient of z⁻¹ in the Taylor product.','For positive k, it is 1/(k−1)!; factorial growth drives the integral magnitude down.'])
m(82,'Complex Analysis',r'pₙ(z)=Σₖ₌₀ⁿzᵏ/k!, n≥1. Which hold?',['pₙ converges locally uniformly to an entire function','Every pₙ has a zero in |z|<2023','There are roots zₙ of pₙ with |zₙ|→∞','The minimum modulus aₙ among roots of pₙ tends to ∞'],'ACD',r'The exponential series converges uniformly on every disk by the M-test, with limit eᶻ, proving A. On |z|≤R, |eᶻ|≥e⁻ᴿ. For sufficiently large n, |pₙ−eᶻ|<e⁻ᴿ/2 throughout that disk, so |pₙ|>0 there. Since R is arbitrary, all roots escape every bounded disk; hence aₙ→∞, proving D. Each degree-n polynomial has roots, and any chosen root then tends in modulus to infinity, proving C. In particular large n have no root in |z|<2023, disproving B.',[('If holomorphic fₙ→1 uniformly on a closed disk, can fₙ have zeros there for arbitrarily large n?','No. Eventually |fₙ−1|<1/2, so |fₙ|≥1/2 throughout the disk.'),('Why does a uniform approximation to eᶻ on |z|≤5 eventually have no zero there?','The exponential modulus is at least e⁻⁵. Error smaller than e⁻⁵/2 gives approximate modulus at least e⁻⁵/2>0.')],['The Taylor polynomials converge locally uniformly to the zero-free exponential.','On each fixed disk eᶻ has a positive minimum modulus.'])
m(83,'Abstract Algebra','Which group-theoretic statements hold?',['Homomorphisms between finite groups of coprime orders are trivial','An endomorphism fixing more than half a finite group fixes everything','A finite group with exactly three subgroups has order p² for a prime p','A finite abelian group has at least d(|G|) subgroups, where d counts positive divisors'],'ABCD',r'A: the image order divides both group orders, so is1. B: fixed points form a subgroup; a proper subgroup has index≥2 and size≤|G|/2, so this subgroup is all G. C: with exactly three subgroups there is one nontrivial proper subgroup H. Any element outside H generates G (its generated subgroup is neither {e} nor H), so G is cyclic. A cyclic group has d(|G|) subgroups, and d(m)=3 iff m=p². D: a finite abelian group has a subgroup of each order dividing its order, by its prime-primary decomposition; distinct orders give at least d(|G|) subgroups.',[('Describe homomorphisms C₃→C₅.','The image order divides3 and5, so is1. Only the trivial homomorphism exists.'),('How many subgroups does C₉ have?','A cyclic group has one subgroup for each divisor1,3,9. There are exactly3.')],['Use Lagrange’s theorem for images and proper-subgroup sizes.','Exactly three subgroups forces cyclicity; finite abelian groups realize every divisor order.'])
m(84,'Number Theory','n≡1 mod7 and n≡4 mod15. Which hold?',['n≡1 mod3','n≡1 mod35','n≡1 mod21','n≡1 mod5'],'AC',r'The mod15 condition implies n≡1 mod3 and n≡4 mod5. Combining n≡1 mod3 with n≡1 mod7 gives n≡1 mod21 since3,7 are coprime. Thus A,C hold and D fails. B would require n≡1 mod5, contradicting n≡4 mod5. Indeed the full solution is n≡64 mod105.',[('Solve n≡1 mod3,n≡1 mod7.','Both n−1 divisibilities combine to21|(n−1), so n≡1 mod21.'),('Solve n≡1 mod7,n≡4 mod15.','Write n=1+7k. Then7k≡3 mod15;7⁻¹≡13 gives k≡9. Thus n=64+105j.')],['Reduce the mod15 condition modulo3 and5.','Combine equal residues modulo coprime3 and7.'])
m(85,'Abstract Algebra',r'G=GL₂(ℤ/9ℤ) has order a. Which hold?',['3⁴ divides a','2⁴ divides a','48 does not divide a','3⁶ divides a'],'AB',r'Reduction modulo3 maps G onto GL₂(F₃). Every invertible matrix modulo3 has3⁴ lifts modulo9, all invertible because their determinants remain nonzero modulo3. |GL₂(F₃)|=(3²−1)(3²−3)=8·6=48. Hence a=3⁴·48=2⁴·3⁵=3888. A,B hold;48 divides a, so C fails; its 3-adic exponent is5, so D fails.',[('Find |GL₂(F₂)|.','Choose a nonzero first column in3 ways and a second outside its 1-dimensional span in2 ways. Total6.'),('Find |GL₂(ℤ/25ℤ)|.','Reduction to F₅ has5⁴ lifts per matrix. |GL₂(F₅)|=(25−1)(25−5)=480, so order5⁴·480=300000.')],['Reduce matrices modulo3 and count lifts.','The reduction kernel has3⁴ elements and GL₂(F₃) has48 elements.'])
m(86,'Abstract Algebra',r'R=ℤ[X]/(X²+1), ψ the quotient map. Which hold?',['R embeds into ℂ as a subring','(ψ(p)) is proper for every prime p','R has infinitely many prime ideals','(ψ(X)) is a prime ideal'],'ABC',r'Evaluation at i has kernel(X²+1), so R≅ℤ[i]⊆ℂ, proving A. R/(p)≅Fₚ[X]/(X²+1) is a nonzero ring, so(p) is proper, proving B. For every rational prime p, choose a maximal ideal above(p). Ideals chosen above distinct primes differ, since containing coprime integers would force1; maximal ideals are prime, proving infinitely many and C. ψ(X)²=−1 makes ψ(X) a unit; its generated ideal is all R, which is not a prime ideal, so D fails.',[('Why is i a unit in ℤ[i]?','i·(−i)=1, so it is invertible. Consequently its principal ideal is the whole ring.'),('Is (3) proper in ℤ[i]?','Yes. The quotient is F₃[X]/(X²+1), a nonzero ring with9 elements. Thus the ideal cannot be the whole ring.')],['Identify the quotient with the Gaussian integers.','The image of X is a unit, while reducing modulo any rational prime gives a nonzero quotient.'])
m(87,'Abstract Algebra',r'f=X²+X+1,g=X²+X−2 in ℤ[X]. Which hold?',['f modulo p is irreducible for every prime p','g modulo some prime p is irreducible','g is irreducible over ℚ','f is irreducible over ℚ'],'D',r'g=(X+2)(X−1), so it is reducible over ℚ and modulo every prime, excluding B,C. f has discriminant1−4=−3, with no rational root, hence is irreducible over ℚ, proving D. Modulo3, f=(X−1)², excluding A.',[('Is X²−2 irreducible over ℚ?','Its roots ±√2 are irrational, so a degree-two polynomial has no rational linear factor and is irreducible.'),('Factor X²+X+1 over F₃.','(X−1)²=X²−2X+1≡X²+X+1 mod3, so it is reducible there.')],['A quadratic is irreducible over a field iff it has no root there.','Factor g over the integers and test f modulo3.'])
m(88,'Abstract Algebra',r'K is the splitting field of X³−2 over ℚ and ω=e^(2πi/3). Which hold?',['Gal(K/ℚ)=S₃','Gal(K/ℚ(ω))=S₃','Gal(K/ℚ)=ℤ/3ℤ','Gal(K/ℚ(ω))=ℤ/3ℤ'],'AD',r'Let α=∛2 real. X³−2 is Eisenstein at2, so [ℚ(α):ℚ]=3. This real field does not contain nonreal ω; adjoining ω gives degree2, hence [K:ℚ]=6. The Galois group acts faithfully on the three roots and has order6, hence is S₃. Over ℚ(ω), the extension has degree3 and is cyclic, generated by α↦ωα with ω fixed. Therefore A,D hold and B,C fail.',[('Find the splitting field degree of X³−5 over ℚ.','The cubic is Eisenstein at5, yielding real degree3. Adjoining a primitive cube root of unity adds degree2, giving6.'),('Why is Gal(K/ℚ(ω)) cyclic of order3?','All roots are α,ωα,ω²α and ω is fixed. Sending α toωα cycles roots and has order3, exhausting the degree-three group.')],['Adjoin both the real cube root and a primitive cube root of unity.','The full extension has degree6; fixing ω leaves a cyclic degree-three extension.'])
T['Topology']=r'''Topology studies continuity and geometric structure through open sets. A subspace inherits intersections of ambient open sets. A space is Hausdorff if distinct points have disjoint neighborhoods; every metric space and its subspaces are Hausdorff. Connectedness means the space cannot be separated into two nonempty disjoint relatively open subsets. Continuous images of connected sets are connected, and connected subsets of the real line are intervals. Compactness means every open cover admits a finite subcover; in Euclidean spaces it is equivalent to closedness and boundedness. Compact subsets of a Hausdorff space are closed. A continuous inclusion sends a compact subspace set to a compact ambient set, so an ambient missing limit point disproves compactness even when the set is relatively closed. Coordinate projections need not send arbitrary closed sets to closed sets.'''
m(89,'Topology',r'ℚ² has the subspace topology from Euclidean ℝ². Which hold?',['ℚ² is connected','Every nonempty connected subset of ℚ² is a singleton','ℚ² is Hausdorff','{(x,y)∈ℚ²:x²+y²=1} is compact'],'BC',r'If a connected subset contained distinct points, at least one coordinate projection would be a connected subset of ℚ with two distinct values. An irrational between them separates that subset, impossible. Thus connected subsets are singletons, proving B and disproving A. The inherited Euclidean metric makes ℚ² Hausdorff, proving C. For D, the rational parametrization ((1−t²)/(1+t²),2t/(1+t²)),t∈ℚ, approaches a point on the circle with irrational coordinates when rational t tends to a suitable irrational (e.g. √2). That missing ambient limit point shows nonclosedness in ℝ², hence noncompactness.',[('Why is ℚ disconnected?','Choose an irrational a. The nonempty sets ℚ∩(−∞,a) and ℚ∩(a,∞) are disjoint relatively open and cover ℚ.'),('Can a set compact in ℚ² fail to be closed in ℝ²?','No. Its inclusion into ℝ² is continuous, so it is compact in ℝ² and therefore closed, because ℝ² is Hausdorff.')],['A coordinate projection separates distinct rational points using an irrational cut.','Relative closedness does not establish compactness; inspect irrational limit points.'])
m(90,'Topology',r'p(x,y)=x. For each of the following sets, is p(A) open in ℝ? Select the true assertions.',['A₁={x²+y²<1}','A₂={x²+y²≤1}','A₃={xy=0}','A₄={xy=1}'],'ACD',r'The images are p(A₁)=(−1,1), p(A₂)=[−1,1], p(A₃)=ℝ (choose y=0), and p(A₄)=ℝ\{0} (choose y=1/x). The first, third and fourth are open. The closed interval is not open: its endpoints have no two-sided neighborhood contained in the interval. Thus A,C,D hold and B fails.',[('Project {(x,y):x²+y²<4} to the x-axis.','An x occurs exactly when |x|<2, so the image is (−2,2), open.'),('Project {(x,y):xy=2} to the x-axis.','Every x≠0 occurs with y=2/x and x=0 never occurs. The image is ℝ\{0}, an open set.')],['Compute each projection as a subset of the real line.','The closed disk projects to a closed interval; the hyperbola omits only zero.'])
m(91,'Ordinary Differential Equations',r'y′=(1−y²)¹⁰cos y,y(0)=0. Let J be the maximal existence interval and K the range. Which hold?',['J=ℝ','K=(−1,1)','J=(−1,1)','K=[−1,1]'],'AB',r'The autonomous right side is smooth, hence locally unique. At y=±1 it vanishes, giving equilibria. Uniqueness prevents a solution starting at0 from reaching either equilibrium at finite time. On (−1,1) the right side is positive, so y is increasing and remains bounded there. A solution trapped in a compact y-region extends for all finite times, giving J=ℝ. Its forward and backward limits must be zeros of the right side in [−1,1], hence1 and−1. These endpoints are never attained, so K=(−1,1).',[('For y′=1−y²,y(0)=0, give J and K.','The solution is tanh x, defined on all ℝ with range(−1,1); the equilibria ±1 are limits, not attained values.'),('Why can a locally unique autonomous solution not reach an equilibrium at finite time?','At the arrival point both the moving solution and the constant equilibrium solve the same initial-value problem. Uniqueness forces them to coincide locally and, by continuation, earlier, a contradiction.')],['The points ±1 are equilibria and the vector field is positive between them.','Uniqueness prevents finite-time contact; boundedness permits global continuation.'])
m(92,'Ordinary Differential Equations',r'y′=y+(1/2)|sin(y²)|,y(0)=−1,x>0. Which hold?',['|y| blows up at some finite positive time','y exists on (0,∞) and is monotone','y exists on (0,∞) but is not bounded below','y exists on (0,∞) but is not bounded above'],'BC',r'The right side is locally Lipschitz and obeys |f(y)|≤|y|+1/2, so finite-time blowup is excluded by a Gronwall bound and the solution is global. For y≤−1, f(y)≤y+1/2≤−1/2, so the solution decreases and never leaves this region upward. Comparison with z′=z+1/2,z(0)=−1 gives y(x)≤−1/2−eˣ/2→−∞. Thus it is monotone and unbounded below but bounded above by−1. B,C hold; A,D fail.',[('Can y′=y+sin y have finite-time blowup?','No. |y+sin y|≤|y|+1, so Gronwall bounds the solution on every finite interval; local existence extends globally.'),('Solve z′=z+1/2,z(0)=−1.','Integrating factor e⁻ˣ gives z+1/2=−eˣ/2, so z=−1/2−eˣ/2, decreasing to−∞.')],['The right side has at most linear growth despite its oscillating term.','For y≤−1 it is negative; compare with y′=y+1/2.'])
m(93,'Ordinary Differential Equations',r'x²y″−2x²y′+(4x−2)y=0,y(0)=0. A polynomial solution φ has φ(1)=1. Which hold?',['φ(4)=16','φ(2)=2','φ(5)=25','φ(3)=3'],'AC',r'If a nonzero polynomial solution has degree d with leading coefficient c, the coefficient of x^(d+1) in the equation is (4−2d)c, so d=2. Write φ=ax²+bx+c₀. The initial value gives c₀=0. Substitution leaves 2b x²−2b x=0, so b=0. φ(1)=1 gives a=1. Thus φ=x², yielding φ(4)=16,φ(2)=4,φ(5)=25,φ(3)=9. A,C hold and B,D fail.',[('Find a polynomial y with y″=2,y(0)=0,y(1)=1.','Integrating gives y=x²+bx+c. Endpoints give c=0,b=0, hence y=x².'),('If a polynomial satisfies x y′=3y and y(1)=2, find it.','For coefficients aₖ, the equation gives (k−3)aₖ=0, so only degree3 remains. The normalization gives y=2x³.')],['Compare the highest polynomial degree before solving coefficients.','The x^(d+1) term forces d=2; then use the two normalization conditions.'])
m(94,'Partial Differential Equations',r'u uₓ+uᵧ=1 on ℝ×(0,∞),u(x,0)=kx. For which k does a classical solution exist on the entire indicated domain?',['k=0','k=−2','k=4','k=1'],'ACD',r'Characteristics starting at (s,0) satisfy du/dy=1 and dx/dy=u. Hence u=ks+y and x=s(1+ky)+y²/2. If1+ky≠0, elimination gives u(x,y)=(kx+y+ky²/2)/(1+ky). For k≥0 this is smooth for every y>0, establishing A,C,D. For k=−2 the factor vanishes at y=1/2; all characteristics meet at x=y²/2=1/8 but carry different u-values, so no single-valued classical solution can extend over that point. B fails.',[('Solve u uₓ+uᵧ=1,u(x,0)=0.','Characteristics have u=y and x=s+y²/2. Thus u(x,y)=y, which directly satisfies the PDE.'),('For initial u(x,0)=−x, locate characteristic crossing.','x=s(1−y)+y²/2. At y=1 all s map to x=1/2, with u=−s+1 differing by s; classical global solvability fails there.')],['Along characteristics, y is the time and u increases at rate1.','The map from the initial coordinate s to x has factor1+ky.'])
m(95,'Partial Differential Equations',r'On the open unit disk B, Sλ consists of C²(B) functions continuous on its closure with uₓ²+λuᵧ²=1 and u=0 on the boundary,λ>0. Which hold?',['S₁=∅','S₂=∅','S₁ has one member and S₂ has two','S₁ and S₂ are both infinite'],'AB',r'A continuous u on the compact closed disk attains a maximum and minimum. If u is nonzero, its positive maximum or negative minimum must occur inside because its boundary values are zero. At that interior extremum the gradient is zero, contradicting uₓ²+λuᵧ²=1. If u is identically zero, the equation is also violated. Thus Sλ is empty for every positive λ, in particular1 and2. A,B hold; C,D fail.',[('Can a C¹ function with zero boundary values on a closed interval satisfy (u′)²=1 everywhere inside?','No. A nonzero function has an interior extremum with u′=0; the zero function also fails the equation.'),('Can u(x,y)=x satisfy uₓ²+2uᵧ²=1?','Yes in the disk, because its derivatives are1 and0, but it does not have zero boundary values. This shows the boundary condition is essential.')],['A nonzero function vanishing on the boundary attains a nonzero extremum inside.','At an interior extremum its gradient vanishes.'])
m(96,'Numerical Analysis',r'For x=0,1,2,3,4 and values y=1,2,1,3,5, find the coefficient of x³ in the interpolating polynomial.',['−1/3','−1/2','5/6','17/6'],'D',r'The forward difference rows begin Δy=(1,−1,2,2),Δ²y=(−2,3,0),Δ³y=(5,−3),Δ⁴y=−8. Newton’s polynomial is 1+x−x(x−1)+(5/6)x(x−1)(x−2)−(1/3)x(x−1)(x−2)(x−3). The cubic product contributes5/6 to x³; the quartic product has cubic coefficient−6 and contributes2. Thus the total coefficient is5/6+2=17/6. The fourth-difference term cannot be omitted.',[('Interpolate values1,2,5 at0,1,2.','First differences1,3 and second difference2 give p=1+x+(2/2)x(x−1)=x²+1.'),('Find the cubic coefficient for values0,1,8,27 at0,1,2,3.','Forward differences are1,7,19; second6,12; third6. The cubic coefficient is6/3!=1, consistent with p=x³.')],['Construct the forward differences for equally spaced nodes.','The degree-four Newton term also contributes to the cubic coefficient.'],extra='Distinct interpolation nodes determine a unique polynomial of degree at most one less than their count. Newton’s divided-difference representation must be fully expanded to obtain a coefficient in the ordinary power basis.')
m(97,'Numerical Analysis',r'A second-order two-stage method is y₁=y₀+Pk₁+Qk₂, k₁=hf(x₀,y₀),k₂=hf(x₀+α₀h,y₀+β₀k₁). Which hold?',['α₀=2 implies β₀=2,P=3/4,Q=1/4','β₀=3 implies α₀=3,P=5/6,Q=1/6','α₀=2 implies β₀=2,P=1/4,Q=3/4','β₀=3 implies α₀=3,P=1/6,Q=5/6'],'AB',r'Taylor expansion gives k₂=hf+h²(α₀fₓ+β₀fᵧf)+O(h³). The exact step is y₀+hf+(h²/2)(fₓ+fᵧf)+O(h³). Matching arbitrary f gives P+Q=1,Qα₀=1/2,Qβ₀=1/2. Hence α₀=β₀ and Q=1/(2α₀). Forα₀=2, Q=1/4,P=3/4; forβ₀=3,Q=1/6,P=5/6. A,B hold; C,D violate the second-order coefficients.',[('Find coefficients for α=β=1 in this scheme.','Order conditions give Q=1/2 and P=1/2, the explicit trapezoidal or Heun method.'),('Find coefficients for α=β=1/2.','Qα=1/2 gives Q=1, and P+Q=1 gives P=0, the explicit midpoint method.')],['Expand both the numerical step and the exact solution through h².','Match independent coefficients of f,fₓ,andfᵧf.'],extra='Order conditions are required for general smooth right-hand sides, not only one specific equation. Consistency alone only enforces P+Q=1; second-order accuracy additionally matches the two second-order derivative contributions.')
m(98,'Calculus of Variations',r'γ connects (1,2) and (2,8) and carries an extremal of J[y]=∫₁²(1+x³y′)y′dx. Which points lie on γ?',['(√2,3)','(√2,6)','(√3,22/3)','(√3,23/3)'],'BC',r'The integrand is L=y′+x³(y′)², independent of y. Euler–Lagrange says d(1+2x³y′)/dx=0, so x³y′ is constant. Integration gives y=A+B/x². Conditions A+B=2 and A+B/4=8 yield B=−8,A=10. Thus y=10−8/x². At√2 it is6, and at√3 it is22/3; B,C hold while A,D fail. Since x³>0 on[1,2], the quadratic derivative term is strictly convex, supporting the unique extremal.',[('Find the fixed-endpoint extremal of ∫₁²x²(y′)²dx.','Euler–Lagrange gives x²y′=C, hence y=A+B/x. Determine A,B from the two endpoint values.'),('For y(1)=0,y(2)=1, solve that example explicitly.','A+B=0,A+B/2=1 imply B=−2,A=2. Thus y=2−2/x.')],['The integrand has no explicit y dependence.','The conserved Euler–Lagrange quantity makes y′ proportional to x⁻³.'])
m(99,'Calculus of Variations',r'S={y∈C¹[0,π]:y(0)=y(π)=0}. B₀(0,ε) uses ‖y‖∞<ε, B₁ uses ‖y‖∞+‖y′‖∞<ε. J[y]=∫₀π(1−(y′)²)y²dx. For some ε>0 which hold?',['J[y]≤J[0] on B₀','J[y]≤J[0] on B₁','J[y]≥J[0] on B₀','J[y]≥J[0] on B₁'],'D',r'J[0]=0. Chooseε<1. In B₁, |y′|<1 so (1−(y′)²)y²≥0 pointwise; D holds. Small y=δsin x has J=πδ²/2−πδ⁴/8>0 and lies in either ball whenδ is small, disproving A,B. For C, take y=δsin(nx), integern, withδ<ε fixed. Its sup norm isδ but J=πδ²/2−πδ⁴n²/8<0 for n>2/δ. Thus no sup-norm-only neighborhood guarantees positivity. Derivative control is essential.',[('For y=δsin(nx), compute ∫₀πy²dx and ∫₀π(y′)²y²dx.','The integrals are πδ²/2 and πδ⁴n²/8, using averages of sin² and sin²cos².'),('If ‖y′‖∞≤1/2, show J[y]≥0.','Then1−(y′)²≥3/4, so J[y]≥(3/4)∫y²dx≥0.')],['In the C¹ ball the derivative is small, making the integrand nonnegative.','High-frequency small-amplitude sine functions defeat control by the sup norm alone.'],d='Hard')
m(100,'Integral Equations',r'y(x)−3∫₀¹tx y(t)dt=f(x). Which continuous f permit at least one solution?',['x²−1/2','eˣ','2−3x','x−1'],'AC',r'Let C=∫₀¹ty(t)dt, so y=f+3xC. Substitution gives C=∫₀¹tf(t)dt+3C∫₀¹t²dt=∫tf+C. Therefore solvability is equivalent to∫₀¹tf(t)dt=0; under this condition any C yields a solution. A gives1/4−1/4=0; B gives∫teᵗdt=1≠0; C gives1−1=0; D gives1/3−1/2=−1/6. Thus A,C qualify.',[('For f=0 in the displayed equation, find all solutions.','Every y=kx solves it:3x∫t·kt dt=3kx/3=kx. These exhaust the rank-one kernel.'),('For f=1, can the displayed equation be solved?','The compatibility integral∫₀¹t dt=1/2 is nonzero, so no solution exists.')],['Reduce the equation to the scalar moment C=∫ty.','At the resonant coefficient3, consistency requires the forcing moment to vanish.'])
m(101,'Integral Equations',r'y(x)=eˣ+∫₀ˣ[(1+x²)/(1+t²)]y(t)dt. Which hold?',['y(1)=(1+π/4)e','y(1)=(1+π/2)e','y(√3)=(1+3π/4)e^√3','y(√3)=(1+4π/3)e^√3'],'BD',r'Put h(x)=y(x)/(1+x²). Then h=eˣ/(1+x²)+∫₀ˣh(t)dt. Differentiating gives h′−h=eˣ[1/(1+x²)−2x/(1+x²)²],h(0)=1. Multiplying by e⁻ˣ and integrating yields e⁻ˣh=arctan x+1/(1+x²). Thus y=eˣ[1+(1+x²)arctan x]. At1 this is(1+π/2)e; at√3 it is(1+4π/3)e^√3. B,D hold, A,C fail.',[('Solve y(x)=1+∫₀ˣy(t)dt.','Differentiate to y′=y,y(0)=1; the unique solution is eˣ, and direct integration verifies it.'),('Solve h′−h=eˣ/(1+x²),h(0)=0.','The integrating factor gives(e⁻ˣh)′=1/(1+x²). Hence h=eˣarctan x.')],['Divide by1+x² to simplify the variable kernel.','Differentiate the resulting Volterra equation and solve the linear ODE using an integrating factor.'])
m(102,'Classical Mechanics',r'Q₁=q₁,P₁=a p₁+16p₂,Q₂=p₂,P₂=2q₁+bq₂ are said to form a canonical transformation. Which assertions hold?',['a²+b²=2','a−b=2','a+b=2','a=1,b=1'],'',r'A canonical transformation must satisfy{Qᵢ,Pⱼ}=δᵢⱼ and{Pᵢ,Pⱼ}={Qᵢ,Qⱼ}=0. Here{Q₁,P₁}=a forcesa=1;{Q₂,P₂}=−b forcesb=−1. However{P₁,P₂}=−2a−16b=14, contradicting the requiredzero. Thus no a,b makes the literal displayed transformation canonical, and the premise is inconsistent. Conditionally, if the coefficient16 were corrected to2, the cross bracket would be−2a−2b=0 ata=1,b=−1; then A,B would hold and C,D wouldfail. That correction is not part of the attached source and is not silently imposed.',[('Is Q=q,P=p+cq canonical in one degree of freedom?','{Q,P}=∂q/∂q·∂(p+cq)/∂p−0=1. The self brackets vanish, so yes for every constantc.'),('For Q₁=q₁,Q₂=p₂,P₁=p₁+2p₂,P₂=2q₁−q₂, check canonicity.','The diagonal mixed brackets are1; cross mixed brackets are0;{P₁,P₂}=−2+2=0;{Q₁,Q₂}=0. Thus the transformation is canonical.')],['Check all fundamental Poisson brackets, including{P₁,P₂}.','The diagonal brackets forcea=1,b=−1; substitute them into the cross momentum bracket.'],extra='The Poisson bracket is{F,G}=Σᵢ(FqᵢGpᵢ−FpᵢGqᵢ). Canonical transformations preserve the entire bracket structure, not just the two diagonal coordinate-momentum brackets.',status='source_issue',note='Exact asset visibly prints16p₂. This makes the canonical premise inconsistent: diagonal brackets requirea=1,b=−1 but the momentum bracket equals14. A,B are only the conditional intended answer under an explicit16→2 correction.')
m(103,'Probability',r'Groups of10 and30 patients have mean diastolic pressures80 and100 and population variances4 and2. For the combined group let X̄,S²,C,R be mean, population variance, percentage coefficient of variation, and range. Which hold?',['X̄=95','S²=77','C>180/19','R>8'],'AD',r'The combined mean is(10·80+30·100)/40=95. The variance is[10(4+(80−95)²)+30(2+(100−95)²)]/40=(2290+810)/40=77.5, so Bfails. C=100√77.5/95=(20/19)√77.5<180/19 since77.5<81, so Cfails. The overall minimum is at most80 and maximum at least100, because each group mean lies between its own extrema. ThusR≥20>8, provingD. A,D hold.',[('Combine two equal-sized groups with means0,2 and population variances1,1.','The mean is1. Combined variance is[(1+1²)+(1+1²)]/2=2, including between-group variation.'),('Two groups have means3 and9. Lower-bound the combined range.','Some value is≤3 and some value≥9, so overall maximum minus minimum is at least6.')],['Use weighted means and include between-group mean shifts in the variance.','Compare√77.5 with9; the means alone give a range bound.'],track='Statistics')
m(104,'Probability',r'X has support{−1,0,1} andP(X=0)=1/3. Which hold?',['E[X]≤2/3','E[X²]=2/3','E[|X|]=2/3','Var(X)>2/3'],'ABC',r'LetP(X=1)=p,P(X=−1)=2/3−p. ThenE[X]=2p−2/3≤2/3, provingA. SinceX²=|X| is1 at±1 and0 at0, both expectations equal2/3, provingB,C. Var(X)=2/3−(E[X])²≤2/3, soD isfalse.',[('For probabilities1/4,1/2,1/4 at−1,0,1, compute variance.','Mean0 and second moment1/2 give variance1/2.'),('For support{−a,0,a} withP(0)=r, findE[X²].','X² equalsa² offzero and0 atzero, soE[X²]=a²(1−r), regardless of how the remaining mass is split.')],['The nonzero support points carry total mass2/3.','UseVar(X)=E[X²]−(E[X])².'],track='Statistics')
m(105,'Stochastic Processes',r'X(t),Y(t) are independent Poisson processes of rate2. LetWₙˣ,Wₙʸ be their nth arrival times. Which hold?',['P(W₂ˣ<W₃ʸ)=11/16','P(W₁ˣ<W₁ʸ)=1/2','P(W₂ˣ<W₃ʸ)=13/16','P(W₁ˣ<W₁ʸ)=1/4'],'AB',r'The merged process has rate4 and independent labelsX,Y with probability1/2 each. The secondX arrives before the thirdY iff at least two of the first four labels areX. Its probability is1−[C(4,0)+C(4,1)]/16=1−5/16=11/16, provingA and excludingC. The first two waiting times are iid continuousExp(2), so by symmetry each is smaller with probability1/2, provingB and excludingD.',[('Equal-rate independent Poisson processes: probability secondX arrives before secondY?','The winner is determined by the majority label among the first3 arrivals. Symmetry gives1/2.'),('Probability firstX arrives before thirdY for equal rates?','The complementary event is the first3 merged labels allY, probability1/8. Thus the answer is7/8.')],['Merge the two processes and use fair independent labels.','SecondX wins against thirdY exactly when at least two of the first four labels areX.'],extra='For independent Poisson processes, superposition adds rates and thinning supplies independent categorical labels with probabilities proportional to rates. Arrival times are continuous, so ties have probabilityzero.',track='Statistics')
m(106,'Probability Distributions',r'P(X=x)=C(25,x)/2²⁵ forx=0,…,25. Which hold?',['X−12.5 and12.5−X have the same distribution','P(X≤4)=P(X≥22)','X has percentage coefficient of variation20','P(X≤4.9)=P(X≥20.1)'],'ACD',r'X∼Bin(25,1/2), symmetric underX↦25−X, provingA. Symmetry givesP(X≤4)=P(X≥21), strictly larger thanP(X≥22) sinceP(X=21)>0, soBfails. Mean12.5 and standard deviation√6.25=2.5 giveCV=100·2.5/12.5=20%, provingC. Integer support convertsX≤4.9 toX≤4 andX≥20.1 toX≥21; these probabilities coincide by symmetry, provingD.',[('ForBin(10,1/2), what upper-tail event matchesX≤2?','UnderX↦10−X, it matchesX≥8. Thus the probabilities are equal.'),('FindCV forBin(16,1/2).','Mean8,variance4,SD2,so percentageCV=100·2/8=25%.')],['Use binomial symmetry around12.5.','Translate noninteger thresholds using the integer support before comparing tails.'],track='Statistics')
m(107,'Probability Distributions',r'X has density1/[π(1+(x+1)²)] on ℝ. LetY=X/|X| forX≠0, andY=0 atX=0. Which hold?',['E[Y]=0','P(Y>0)<P(Y<0)','P(Y<−1)<P(Y>1)','E[Y²]=1'],'BD',r'X is Cauchy with location−1 and scale1, with cdfF(x)=1/2+arctan(x+1)/π. ThusP(X≤0)=3/4 andP(X>0)=1/4. SinceX=0 has probabilityzero, Y takes−1 with probability3/4 and1 with probability1/4. Its mean is−1/2, excludingA; its positive probability is smaller than its negative probability, provingB. Both eventsY<−1,Y>1 have probabilityzero, so the strict comparisonCfails. Y²=1 almost surely, provingD.',[('IfP(X>0)=.6 andP(X=0)=0, findE[sgn X].','The sign is1 with probability.6 and−1 with probability.4, so expectation.6−.4=.2.'),('For standard CauchyX, findE[(sgn X)²].','The continuous law gives no mass atzero; the squared sign is1 almost surely, so its expectation is1 despite the original Cauchy mean being undefined.')],['Integrate the Cauchy density or use its arctangent cdf.','Y has only two almost-sure values,1 and−1.'],track='Statistics')
m(108,'Probability Distributions',r'U∼Uniform(0,1),X=tan(π(U−1/2)). Which hold?',['E[X⁴]=3','P(X∈{1,2,5})=1/2','E[eˣ] does not exist as a finite expectation','P(X≤0)=1/2'],'CD',r'The monotone transformation givesF(x)=1/2+arctan x/π and density1/[π(1+x²)], the standard Cauchy law. Its fourth moment diverges becausex⁴/(1+x²) grows quadratically, soAfails. A continuous law assigns probabilityzero to the finite set{1,2,5}, soBfails. The integral ofeˣ/[π(1+x²)] over the positive tail diverges; no finite exponential moment exists, provingC. Symmetry orU≤1/2 givesP(X≤0)=1/2, provingD.',[('Find the cdf of tan(π(U−1/2)).','Because tangent is increasing on(−π/2,π/2), P(X≤x)=P(U≤1/2+arctan x/π), yielding the Cauchy cdf.'),('Does standard Cauchy have a finite second moment?','No. Its second-moment integrandx²/[π(1+x²)] tends to1/π in either tail, so its integral diverges.')],['Recognize the inverse-cdf construction of a standard Cauchy variable.','Cauchy tails are polynomial, so neither the fourth nor positive exponential moment is finite.'],track='Statistics')
m(109,'Statistical Inference',r'For iidN(μ,1), testμ=μ₀ againstμ>μ₀. TestA rejects whenX̄ₙ>c₁ and testB when the sample median>c₂, each calibrated to sizeα. Which hold?',['TestA is UMP of sizeα','TestB is UMP of sizeα','Pμ(X̄ₙ>c₁)→1 for everyμ>μ₀','Pμ₀(median>μ₀)=1/2'],'ACD',r'The normal likelihood ratio against anyμ₁>μ₀ is strictly increasing inΣXᵢ. Neyman–Pearson therefore gives the mean-tail region as UMP, provingA. Its cutoff isc₁=μ₀+z₁₋α/√n; at fixedμ>μ₀ its standardized alternative cutoff tends to−∞, so power tends to1, provingC. Reflection of every observation aboutμ₀ preserves the null law and reflects the median; continuity gives no equality mass, provingD. For generaln≥3 the median rejection region differs from the unique likelihood-ratio optimum, soBisfalse. Forn=1,2 the conventional median equals the mean, so the two tests coincide; B is not a universal assertion in n.',[('For known variance4 andn=16, give a size.05 upper-tail mean test cutoff.','SE=2/4=.5, so cutoff=μ₀+.5z.95. Under a fixed higher mean, power is1−Φ((cutoff−μ)/.5).'),('Why is the median of a continuous symmetric sample equally likely above or below the center?','Reflecting all observations maps each sample to an equally likely sample with median mirrored about the center. Equality has probabilityzero, so the two probabilities are1/2.')],['The normal likelihood ratio is monotone in the sample sum.','Use shrinking mean standard error for consistency and null reflection symmetry for the median.'],track='Statistics',note='General-sample answer A,C,D. At n=1 or2 the conventional median equals the mean and B also holds; the universal UMP comparison is interpreted for general n, as in the final key.')
m(110,'Statistical Inference',r'For25 iidBernoulli(p), X̄=ΣXᵢ/25. On0<X̄<1 letT₁=5(X̄−.5)/√(X̄(1−X̄)), withT₁=−5 at0 and5 at1; T₂=10(X̄−.5). Testsψᵢ reject iffTᵢ>2. If observedX̄∈(.5,.75), which implications hold?',['ψ₁ rejection impliesψ₂ rejection','ψ₁ nonrejection impliesψ₂ nonrejection','ψ₂ rejection impliesψ₁ rejection','ψ₂ nonrejection impliesψ₁ nonrejection'],'ABCD',r'Writeδ=X̄−.5>0. T₁>2 iff25δ²>4(.25−δ²), equivalentlyδ>1/√29 orX̄>.685695…. T₂>2 iffX̄>.7. HoweverX̄ is a sample proportion from25 trials, so its only possible values in(.5,.75) are.52,.56,.60,.64,.68,.72. Both tests reject exactly at.72 and accept at the other five. Hence their decisions coincide and all four implications hold literally. The intended continuous-threshold comparison gives onlyB,C because T₁≥T₂ and a continuous value such as.69 would rejectψ₁ but notψ₂. Such a value cannot occur for25 Bernoulli trials. The attached question therefore conflicts with that intended comparison; this entry preserves the lattice analysis rather than inventing an attainable counterexample.',[('For25 Bernoulli trials, can the observed proportion be.69?','No.25·.69=17.25 is not an integer number of successes; possible proportions are integer multiples of.04.'),('Treatq as a free real value. Atq=.69 compare the two displayed tests.','T₂=1.9≤2, whileT₁=.95/√(.69·.31)≈2.054>2. Thus continuous-threshold reasoning separates the tests, but this q is unattainable in the stated sample.')],['Compute each rejection threshold, then remember the finite sample-proportion lattice.','There is no attainable value between.685695… and.7 for a25-trial sample.'],track='Statistics',status='source_issue',note='Literal sample lattice makes all four implications true. The continuous comparison, yielding B,C in the official key, requires an unattainable mean between .685695… and .7. Full exact-threshold and attainable-value diagnosis retained.')
m(111,'Statistical Inference',r'Xᵢ have densitye^(θ−x) forx≥θ and0 otherwise,θ∈ℝ. LetX̄ be the mean andX₍₁₎ the minimum. Which hold?',['X̄ is the method-of-moments estimator ofθ','X₍₁₎ is the MLE ofθ','X₍₁₎−1/n is the UMVUE ofθ','X₍₁₎ is sufficient forθ'],'BCD',r'Xᵢ=θ+Exp(1), soE[Xᵢ]=θ+1 and the moments estimator isX̄−1, notX̄. The likelihood isexp(nθ−Σxᵢ)1{θ≤x₍₁₎}, increasing over its allowable θ-values; its maximum occurs atθ=x₍₁₎, provingB. Factorization proves minimum sufficiency, givingD. X₍₁₎−θ isExp(n), soX₍₁₎−1/n is unbiased. The minimum is also complete: if∫θ∞g(t)ne⁻ⁿ⁽ᵗ⁻θ⁾dt=0 for everyθ, multiplying bye⁻ⁿθ and differentiating the resulting tail integral forcesg(θ)=0 almost everywhere. Lehmann–Scheffé provesC.',[('For one shiftedExp(1) observationX, find an unbiased estimator ofθ.','SinceE[X]=θ+1, X−1 is unbiased.'),('For4 shiftedExp(1) observations, find the MLE and its bias.','The MLE is the minimum. Its excess aboveθ isExp(4), so bias=1/4; subtract1/4 for an unbiased estimator.')],['The model is a location shift of an exponential law.','The likelihood’s support constraint boundsθ by the sample minimum; that minimum’s mean excess is1/n.'],track='Statistics')
m(112,'Statistical Inference',r'IidN(μ,σ²),n≥2,μ andσ unknown. S²=Σ(Xᵢ−X̄)²/(n−1). zα,tₘ,α denote1−α quantiles. Which are90% confidence intervals forμ?',['(X̄−S tₙ₋₁,.₀₅/√n,X̄+S tₙ₋₁,.₀₅/√n)','(X̄−σz.₀₅/√n,X̄+σz.₀₅/√n)','[X̄−S tₙ₋₁,.₉/√n,∞)','(−∞,X̄−S tₙ₋₁,.₉/√n)'],'AD',r'T=√n(X̄−μ)/S has tₙ₋₁ distribution. A is the usual central90% interval, since its two tails have probability.05 each. B contains the unknownσ, so is not a data-computable confidence interval in the stated unknown-variance problem, although it would be valid ifσ were known. Letq=tₙ₋₁,.9, the lower.1 quantile, which is negative. C coversμ iffT≤q, with probability.1 rather than.9. D coversμ iffT≥q, with probability.9, so it is a valid one-sided90% upper interval. ThusA,D.',[('Give a95% interval for a normal mean with unknown variance.','UseX̄±S tₙ₋₁,.025/√n, since the Student pivot has a central.95 probability between its .025 and .975 quantiles.'),('Give a90% upper confidence bound forμ.','Using lower.1 Student quantileq<0, U=X̄−qS/√n. ThenP(μ≤U)=P(T≥q)=.9.')],['Use the Student pivot becauseσ is unknown.','tₙ₋₁,.9 is the lower10% quantile, hence negative; check each one-sided coverage inequality.'],track='Statistics')
m(113,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'Three iid bivariate normal pairs have mean0, variances1 and correlationρ,|ρ|<1. Sρ=3E[sgn((X₁−X₂)(Y₁−Y₃))]. Which hold?',['Independence ofX₁,Y₁ impliesSρ=0','Sρ=(6/π)arcsin(ρ/2)','Sρ=0 implies independence ofX₁,Y₁','Independence impliesSρ=1/2'],'ABC',r'A=X₁−X₂ andB=Y₁−Y₃ have variances2 and covarianceρ, because only the pair(X₁,Y₁) contributes. Their correlation isr=ρ/2. For a centered bivariate normal pair, P(A>0,B>0)=1/4+arcsin r/(2π), soE[sgn(AB)]=4P(A>0,B>0)−1=(2/π)arcsin r. ThereforeSρ=(6/π)arcsin(ρ/2), provingB. It vanishes iffρ=0; zero correlation is equivalent to independence for a bivariate normal pair. ThusA,C hold andD fails.',[('For centered unit-variance normalA,B with correlation1/2, findE[sgn(AB)].','The arcsine identity gives(2/π)arcsin(1/2)=(2/π)(π/6)=1/3.'),('For independent continuous symmetricA,B, findP(AB>0).','Same-sign events have probabilities1/4 each, totaling1/2; opposite signs also total1/2, so expected product sign is0.')],['Compute covariance and variance of the two differences.','Use the bivariate normal quadrant-probability arcsine formula.'],extra='For completeness, the quadrant identity can be derived by representing B=rA+√(1−r²)Z with independent standard normals and interpreting the angular wedge in the rotationally symmetric (A,Z) plane.',track='Statistics')
m(114,'Statistical Inference',r'Fₙ is the empirical cdf, F₀ an absolutely continuous specified cdf. LetT₁,ₙ=supₓ|Fₙ(x)−F₀(x)| andT₂,ₙ=nT₁,ₙ. Which hold?',['T₁,ₙ→0 in probability underH₀:F=F₀','T₂,ₙ→0 in probability underH₀','P_F(T₂,ₙ>1)→1 for every absolutely continuousF','T₂,ₙ has a degenerate real-valued distributional limit underH₀'],'AC',r'Glivenko–Cantelli givesT₁,ₙ→0 almost surely underH₀, provingA. Under continuousH₀,√nT₁,ₙ converges to the nondegenerate Kolmogorov law, which is positive almost surely. Hence nT₁,ₙ→∞ in probability: for anyM, the thresholdM/√n in the √n-scaled statistic tends to0. This excludesB and any finite degenerate real limit inD. ForF≠F₀, uniform convergence ofFₙ toF givesT₁,ₙ→‖F−F₀‖∞>0, soT₂,ₙ→∞. Combining null and alternative cases provesC for every statedF.',[('Under a fixed alternative with‖F−F₀‖∞=.2, what happens to the KS statistic?','The unscaled statistic converges almost surely to.2, while multiplying it byn makes it diverge to∞.'),('Under a continuous null, what is the appropriate KS scaling?','√n times the supremum discrepancy has a nondegenerate Brownian-bridge supremum limit. Scaling byn is larger by√n and diverges.')],['Glivenko–Cantelli handles the unscaled discrepancy.','Under the null the fluctuation scale is n⁻¹/², so multiplication byn diverges rather than vanishes.'],extra='The empirical distribution function isFₙ(x)=n⁻¹Σ1{Xᵢ≤x}. Under a continuous null, the Kolmogorov–Smirnov limit is sup|B(t)| for a Brownian bridgeB on[0,1], with no atom atzero.',track='Statistics')
m(115,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'Yᵢⱼ=μ+αᵢ+εᵢⱼ, j=1,…,nᵢ,i=1,…,k, errors uncorrelated with mean0 and common positive variance. No identifying constraint is stated. Which hold?',['The grand sample mean is unbiased forμ','2μ+α₁+α₂ is estimable','μ+α₁+α₂ is estimable','The mean of(Y₂ⱼ−Ȳ₂) is unbiased forα₂'],'B',r'Only the group meansmᵢ=μ+αᵢ are identifiable. Replacingμ byμ+c and allαᵢ byαᵢ−c leaves every data expectation unchanged. A general functionc₀μ+Σcᵢαᵢ is estimable iffc₀=Σcᵢ. B satisfies this and is unbiasedly estimated byȲ₁+Ȳ₂. C fails the condition. The grand mean has expectationμ+Σnᵢαᵢ/Σnᵢ, not necessarilyμ, excludingA. The centered within-group mean inD is identicallyzero, so cannot estimate unrestrictedα₂.',[('Isα₁−α₂ estimable in the unrestricted model?','Yes. Its coefficients satisfy0=1−1, andȲ₁−Ȳ₂ is unbiased for it.'),('Isμ alone estimable without a constraint?','No. Shiftingμ byc and eachα by−c preserves all data distributions but changesμ; no estimator can identify it.')],['Find the parameter shift leaving every group mean unchanged.','An estimable linear function must be invariant under that shift.'],track='Statistics')
m(116,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'Y=Xβ+ε, X is full rankn×(p+1),n>p+1,ε∼N(0,σ²I). For OLSβ̂, which hold?',['YᵀXβ̂/σ² has a centralχ²ₚ₊₁ law','Residual sum of squares/σ² has centralχ²ₙ₋ₚ₋₁ law','Xβ̂ and residual sum of squares are independent','Σ(Yᵢ−Ȳ)²/σ² has centralχ²ₙ₋₁ law'],'BC',r'LetH=X(XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀ. Fitted values areHY and residuals(I−H)Y. The residual mean iszero because(I−H)Xβ=0, andI−H is an orthogonal projection of rankn−p−1, so its squared norm/σ² is centralχ²ₙ₋ₚ₋₁, provingB. Fitted and residual Gaussian vectors have zero cross-covarianceσ²H(I−H)=0, hence are independent; this extends to residual squared norm, provingC. YᵀXβ̂=YᵀHY generally has noncentrality‖Xβ‖²/σ², excludingA. Corrected total sum of squares is generally noncentral when fitted means vary across observations, excludingD.',[('IfH projects onto a2-dimensional subspace andY∼N(0,I₅), what isYᵀHY?','An orthogonal change of coordinates makes it the sum of two independent standard-normal squares, henceχ²₂.'),('Why areHY and(I−H)Y independent for GaussianY?','Their covariance isHΣ(I−H); withΣ=σ²I this iszero. Jointly Gaussian vectors with zero cross-covariance are independent.')],['Use the orthogonal projection onto the design’s column space.','Residuals have zero mean; fitted and total sums can have noncentrality under nonzeroβ.'],track='Statistics')
m(117,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'A=(aᵢⱼ)∼W₃(5,Σ),Σ=[[2,1,1],[1,2,0],[1,0,2]]. Which hold?',['a₂₂∼χ²₃','a₂₂/2∼χ²₅','(a₁₁−4a₁₃+4a₃₃)/33∼χ²₃','(a₁₁−4a₁₃+4a₃₃)/9∼χ²₅'],'B',r'For WishartWₚ(ν,Σ), vᵀAv/(vᵀΣv)∼χ²ν for any nonzero deterministicv. Atv=e₂, the scale isΣ₂₂=2, soa₂₂/2∼χ²₅, provingB and excludingA. Forv=(1,0,−2), vᵀAv=a₁₁−4a₁₃+4a₃₃ andvᵀΣv=2−4·1+4·2=6. Thus division by6, not9 or33, givesχ²₅; C,D fail.',[('ForA∼W₂(7,diag(3,4)), find the distribution ofa₁₁/3.','Usev=e₁: vᵀΣv=3 andν=7. Hencea₁₁/3∼χ²₇.'),('ForΣ=[[1,.5],[.5,2]],v=(1,−1), compute the Wishart quadratic scale.','vᵀΣv=1−2·.5+2=2. Thus(a₁₁−2a₁₂+a₂₂)/2∼χ²ν.')],['Wishart degrees of freedom remain5 for every fixed quadratic projection.','EvaluatevᵀΣv forv=(1,0,−2); its value is6.'],extra='The Wishart representationA=Σⱼ₌₁νZⱼZⱼᵀ with independentNₚ(0,Σ) vectors makes each normalized quadratic form the sum ofν squared standard normals.',track='Statistics')
m(118,'Sampling and Design of Experiments',r'A simple random sample without replacement of2 units is drawn from population values2,4,6. LetM be its mean. Which hold?',['E[M]=4','E[M²]=17','E[M³]=72','Var(M)=1'],'AC',r'The three equally likely samples are{2,4},{2,6},{4,6}, with means3,4,5. HenceE[M]=(3+4+5)/3=4, provingA. E[M²]=(9+16+25)/3=50/3, not17. E[M³]=(27+64+125)/3=72, provingC. Var(M)=50/3−16=2/3, not1. ThusA,C are true.',[('Draw2 without replacement from1,3,5. Find mean and variance of the sample mean.','Possible means2,3,4 are equally likely. Mean3; variance[(−1)²+0²+1²]/3=2/3.'),('Draw1 unit uniformly from2,4,6. Find variance.','Mean4; variance[(2−4)²+0+(6−4)²]/3=8/3, larger than the two-unit sample-mean variance.')],['Enumerate the three equally likely unordered samples.','Their sample means are3,4,5; compute each moment directly.'],track='Statistics')
m(119,'Probability Distributions',r'Independent component lifetimesXᵢ have densitiesi e⁻ⁱˣ forx≥0,i=1,2. X is the lifetime of their series system. Which hold?',['P(X>4)=P(X>1)P(X>2)','P(X>4|X>2)=P(X>2)','E[X]=1/3','6X∼χ²₃'],'BC',r'A series system fails when either component fails, soX=min(X₁,X₂). Independence givesP(X>x)=e⁻ˣe⁻²ˣ=e⁻³ˣ; X∼Exp(3). A compares e⁻¹² withe⁻⁹ and isfalse. Memorylessness givesP(X>4|X>2)=e⁻⁶=P(X>2), provingB. The mean is1/3, provingC. Multiplying by6 givesExp(rate1/2), which isχ²₂ rather thanχ²₃, excludingD.',[('For independentExp(2),Exp(5) lifetimes in series, find the system mean.','The minimum has survival e⁻⁷ˣ, henceExp(7) and mean1/7.'),('IfX∼Exp(λ), what is2λX distributed as?','Its survival is e⁻ʸ/², so it has exponential rate1/2, equivalent toχ²₂.')],['A series lifetime is the minimum of its independent component lifetimes.','The minimum is exponential with rate1+2=3; apply memorylessness and theχ²₂ gamma identity.'],track='Statistics')
m(120,'Stochastic Processes',r'An M/M/1 queue has arrival rate15/hour and service rate45/hour. LetN(t) be system size andT₁,T₂ stationary queue and system times. Which hold?',['P(N(t)=1)→2/9','P(T₁>0)=1/3','E[T₁]=1/90 hours','E[T₂]=1/35 hours'],'ABC',r'The traffic intensityρ=λ/μ=1/3<1, so a stationary regime exists withπₙ=(1−ρ)ρⁿ. Thusπ₁=(2/3)(1/3)=2/9, provingA. By PASTA an arrival waits with probability the server is busy,ρ=1/3, provingB. Mean system time is1/(μ−λ)=1/30 hour; subtract mean service1/μ=1/45 gives mean queue time1/30−1/45=1/90 hour, provingC and excludingD. TheT-statements use the conventional stationary-customer interpretation.',[('An M/M/1 hasλ=4,μ=6 per hour. Find mean system and queue times.','ρ=2/3; system mean1/(6−4)=1/2 hour. Queue meanρ/(μ−λ)=(2/3)/2=1/3 hour.'),('Forλ=2,μ=5, find stationary probability of zero customers.','ρ=2/5 andπ₀=1−ρ=3/5.')],['Computeρ and confirm stabilityλ<μ.','Use the geometric stationary law andW=1/(μ−λ),Wq=W−1/μ.'],extra='M/M/1 means Poisson arrivals, independent exponential services, and one server. PASTA states that Poisson arrivals see the same state probabilities as time averages in stationarity. Queue time excludes service time; system time includes it.',track='Statistics')
